          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The identity morphism of an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cospan
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\mathrm{Cospan}
              is simply
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \overset{\mathrm{id}}\to x \overset{\mathrm{id}}\leftarrow x
              equipped with the empty decoration on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#BrendanFong2015"} B. Fong 2015, Appendix A
          .un_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p The composition of decorations is the key construction of the decorated cospans formalisms. In fact, returning to the analogy with open networks, it constructs the composite network of a given ‘link’ of two networks. Hence the compositional structures of the cospans is leveraged to describe the compositional structure of a richer structure.
          .un_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Notice that the empty decoration gives a canonical way to decorate any cospan on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf C
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cospan
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi F
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cospan
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Cospan}(\mathbf C) \embedsin F\mathrm{Cospan}
          %h3#the_monoidal_and_hypergraph_structures The monoidal and hypergraph structures
          %p
            In the presence of a braiding on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf D
            (the ‘decorating category’), the category of decorated cospans becomes not just
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypergraph+category"} hypergraph category
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#BrendanFong2015"}> B. Fong, 2015
              ) Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf C
              be a category with finite colimts,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf D, \otimes)
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal category
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F, \phi) : (\mathbf C, +) \to (\mathbf D, \otimes)
              a lax braided monoidal functor. Then we may equip
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cospan
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\mathrm{Cospan}
              with a symmetric monoidal and hypergraph structure, such that there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wide+subcategory"} wide embedding
              of hypergraph categories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cospan
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi F
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cospan
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathrm{Cospan}(\mathbf C) \embedsin F\mathrm{Cospan}.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              We define the monoidal product of objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cospan
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\mathrm{Cospan}
              to be their coproduct
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x+y
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi i
                    %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mi o
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi s
                    %mi F
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \overset{i}\to n \overset{o}\leftarrow y, 1 \overset{s}\to F n)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi i
                    %mi n
                    %mo ′
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mi o
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo ′
                    %mi F
                    %mi n
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \overset{i}\to n' \overset{o}\leftarrow y, 1 \overset{s'}\to F n')
              to be
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mi n
                            %mo ′
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi i
                                  %mi x
                                %mo +
                                %msub
                                  %mi i
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi x
                                    %mo ′
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↖
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi o
                                  %mi y
                                %mo +
                                %msub
                                  %mi o
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi y
                                    %mo ′
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ,
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi λ
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                    %mn 1
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mn 1
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mi s
                                %mo ⊗
                                %mi s
                                %mo ′
                            %mi F
                            %mi n
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi F
                            %mi n
                            %mo ′
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi ϕ
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi n
                                    %mo ,
                                    %mi n
                                    %mo ′
                            %mi F
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mi n
                            %mo ′
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi x
                            %mo +
                            %mi x
                            %mo ′
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi y
                            %mo +
                            %mi y
                            %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; n + n'
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}^{i_x + i_{x'}}\nearrow
                    \&amp;&amp; \nwarrow^{o_y + o_{y'}} &amp;&amp;,&amp;&amp; 1 \overset{\lambda^{-1}}\longrightarrow 1 \otimes 1 \overset{s \otimes s'}\longrightarrow F n \otimes F n' \overset{\phi_{n,n'}}\longrightarrow F(n+n')
                    \\\
                    x+x'
                    \&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;
                    y+y'
                    }
            %p
              The braiding in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf D
              can be now used to show this product is indeed functorial. Finally, we choose associator, unitors and braiding to be the images of those in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cospan
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Cospan}(\mathbf C)
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#BrendanFong2015"} B. Fong 2015, Appendix A
            %p
              The hypergraph structure is defined by equipping each object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cospan
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in F\mathrm{Cospan}
              with the image of the special commutative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebra"} Frobenius monoid
              specified by the hypergraph structure of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cospan
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Cospan}(\mathbf C)
          %p
            If the monoidal unit of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf D, \otimes)
            is the initial object, then each object admits only a decoration — the empty one. This implies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cospan
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Cospan}(\mathbf C)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cospan
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{\mathbf C}\mathrm{Cospan}
            are isomorphic as hypergraph categories.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+cospan"} structured cospan
            %li
